<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Family and I</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> It's My Family!</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use pictures or objects to express ideas. Communication: Use gestures and demonstrations to help classmates perform a simple task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use pictures and gestures to describe their family to classmates (e.g., miming or displaying a drawing or picture of a family member). Can clarify responses by pointing to family pictures or objects (e.g., pointing to a picture of their mother). Can use simple gestures or facial expressions to show who they are talking about in their family (e.g., pointing to a family member's picture).</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; text-align: left;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Stage & Description</h3></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; text-align: left;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Activities & Teacher Actions</h3></th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f0f0; text-align: left;"><h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Estimated Date and Time</h3></th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Family Song" with actions. As the song plays, the teacher holds up large flashcards of "mom," "dad," "brother," "sister," etc. Students point to the corresponding family member on the flashcard or to their own chest if it's "I love my family." This activates prior knowledge and introduces visuals for mediation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple drawing or picture of their own family. While pointing to each person, the teacher models: "This is my mom." (points, smiles), "She is friendly." (gestures friendly face). "This is my dad." (points), "He is tall." (gestures height). The teacher demonstrates using the visual aid and simple gestures to explain.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'sister' and asks: "Is this a boy or a girl?" (Expected answer: "Girl"). The teacher then holds up a 'dad' flashcard and asks: "Is 'dad' a boy or a girl?" (Expected answer: "Boy"). The teacher points to a picture of a mom and asks: "Who is she?" (Expected answer: "Mom").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A large, colorful "My Family" poster showing simple drawings of different family members (mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather, pet) with their names labeled below the pictures. The poster also includes simple sentences like "She is my mom." or "He is my dad." The teacher uses flashcards for individual family members.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Introducing family members: Use simple sentences to describe family (e.g., "She is my mom."). Asking about family: Respond to basic questions about family members (e.g., "Who is he?" "He is my brother.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher points to a family member on the poster and asks, "Who is this?" Students respond chorally or individually: "Mom!" or "She is my mom."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher says a family member's name (e.g., "Show me grandfather!"), and students point to the correct picture on the poster or flashcard.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher asks, "Is she tall or short?" while pointing to a picture, and students use gestures or words to respond.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b>
                Grammar: Personal pronouns + verb ("She is," "He is"), Possessive adjectives ("my mom," "my dad"), WH-questions ("Who is she/he?").
                Vocabulary: mom, dad, brother, sister, grandmother, grandfather, pet, friendly, tall, short.
                Pronunciation: /m/ in "mom," /d/ in "dad," /b/ in "brother," /s/ in "sister."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: "Match and Say."</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of family members and a word bank. They draw lines to match the picture to the correct word (e.g., picture of 'mom' to the word 'mom'). Then, individually or chorally, they point to a matched picture and say, "This is my mom." or "She is my sister." The teacher monitors pronunciation of /m/, /d/, /b/, /s/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "Picture Description."</b> The teacher holds up a large flashcard of a family member (e.g., 'grandfather'). Students complete sentence frames displayed on the board (e.g., "He is my _______." "He is _______."). The teacher prompts for adjectives, e.g., "Is he old or young?" Students point to the picture and say, "He is my grandfather. He is old." The teacher encourages using gestures to describe adjectives (e.g., stretching up for 'tall', bending down for 'short').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Guess My Family Member."</b> In pairs, one student secretly picks a family member flashcard. They then use gestures and simple facial expressions (without speaking the name) to describe that family member (e.g., miming cooking for 'mom,' miming playing for 'brother,' miming hugging for 'friendly'). Their partner tries to guess, "Is it your mom?" "Is she friendly?" The first student responds "Yes, it is my mom!" or "No, it is my brother." This develops non-verbal mediation and simple question-and-answer skills.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Family and I".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Family Portrait Presentation."</b> Students will present their previously drawn "Family Portrait" (21st Century Skills Project) to a small group of classmates (3-4 students). Each student takes turns being the presenter.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students sit in small groups with their "Family Portraits."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A displays their portrait. They point to each family member in their drawing and say, "This is my mom." (points to mom). "She is friendly." (smiles, gestures). "This is my dad." (points to dad). "He is tall." (gestures height). They can also point to a pet and say, "This is my pet."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Other students in the group can ask simple WH-questions: "Who is he?" or "Who is she?" pointing to a family member in the portrait. Student A responds, "He is my brother." or "She is my grandmother." while pointing to clarify.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, observing and offering support, encouraging students to use gestures and pointing as a key mediation strategy. The focus is on communication and using the picture/drawing as a tool to convey information.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h4>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "Family Portrait" (from Stage 4) to the teacher or a peer, actively using gestures and pointing to clarify who each person is and responding to "Who is he/she?" questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can use pictures (family portrait) and gestures/pointing to describe at least 3 family members.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses "This is my mom/dad/brother/sister" and "He is/She is" correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to basic "Who is he/she?" questions by pointing and stating the family member.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Pronunciation of target vocabulary (mom, dad, brother, sister) is intelligible.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Actively collaborates and listens to classmates' presentations.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a quick drawing or circle an emoji:
                "I can show my family picture and say 'This is my mom'." (Circle happy face / neutral face / sad face)
                "I can ask 'Who is he?'" (Draw a thumbs up / thumbs down)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h4 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h4>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was easy about showing your family picture to your friends? (Students can draw or point to an emoji).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was difficult about telling your friends about your family? (Students can draw or point to an emoji).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students use pictures and gestures to mediate information about their family? (e.g., the "Family Portrait" project, the "Guess My Family Member" game).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific challenges did students face in using gestures or simple sentences to clarify their family members? (e.g., remembering possessive adjectives, consistent pointing, responding to WH-questions).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation activities at this level? (e.g., more direct modeling of gestures, sentence starters for asking questions).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "My Family and I" theme, integrating all learned skills through the family portrait project. The next lesson will introduce a new theme, possibly focusing on describing objects or actions in a different context, building upon the foundational language and mediation skills practiced here.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>